"use strict";exports.id=2711,exports.ids=[2711],exports.modules={62711:(e,t,o)=>{o.d(t,{TRACKS:()=>a});let i={orange:"#E8621A",gold:"#C9A84C",purple:"#A855F7",teal:"#0F505A"},a=[{id:"common-sense-in-the-workplace",num:"01",tag:"Foundation",title:"Common Sense in the Workplace",subtitle:"Awareness \xb7 Communication \xb7 Vibe Hygiene",color:i.orange,totalTime:"~2.5 hrs",description:"The foundational track. Before de-escalation, self-mastery, or team cohesion is possible, every professional needs a baseline level of emotional awareness, communication clarity, and personal vibe hygiene.",lessons:[{id:"b01-l01",num:"01",title:"The Awareness Gap",duration:"20 min",type:"video",description:"Most workplace friction isn't caused by bad people — it's caused by unawareness of the distance between intention and experienced impact.",objectives:["Define the Awareness Gap and why it generates the majority of preventable professional friction","Distinguish clearly between internal intent, observable behavior, and interpersonal impact","Identify your default self-monitoring baseline when cortisol rises under workplace stress"],content:["The Awareness Gap is the distance between who you think you're being and who others actually experience. Most workplace friction isn't caused by bad people—it's caused by unawareness.","We operate from intention ('I was just being direct and efficient') while others receive behavior ('that comment felt dismissive and rushed'). Neither is wrong—both are real. The professional who closes this gap becomes someone others genuinely want to collaborate with.","Neuroscience tells us that under stress, the prefrontal cortex—responsible for self-monitoring, perspective-taking, and nuanced tone regulation—goes offline first as blood flow redirects to survival circuits. Emotional intelligence is the skill of keeping your self-monitoring system online when it matters most.","Your Awareness Inventory: For the next 48 hours, practice a single question after every meaningful interaction—'What did they experience from me?' Not what you intended. What they actually received through your tone, pace, and posture."],tool:"Awareness Inventory Log",lab:{title:"Awareness Gap Audit",subtitle:"Impact vs. Intent",scenario:"You gave 'direct feedback' to a colleague or direct report during a busy afternoon, intending to be helpful and efficient. They went quiet, offered a one-word reply, and withdrew for the rest of the day.",prompt:"What subtle 'threat signals' (vocal clipped pace, lack of eye contact, physical tension, abrupt timing) were you potentially broadcasting that overrode your helpful intention—and how would you re-open that conversation tomorrow?",accentColor:i.orange},quiz:[{q:"The Awareness Gap describes the distance between:",options:["Your internal intentions and how others actually experience your behavior","Your technical skill level and your manager's quarterly expectations","What you write in emails versus what you say in live meetings","Your current job title and the leadership role you want next"],correct:0,explanation:"We judge ourselves by our internal intentions, while colleagues only experience our observable behavior (tone, pace, word choice, posture). That distance is the Awareness Gap."},{q:"Under workplace stress and elevated cortisol, which brain region goes offline first?",options:["The Amygdala (threat detection center)","The Cerebellum (motor coordination)","The Prefrontal Cortex (self-monitoring and perspective-taking)","The Brainstem (autonomic arousal)"],correct:2,explanation:"The prefrontal cortex is metabolically expensive and is the first region to lose regulatory control under stress, which is why self-awareness drops right when pressure rises."},{q:"What is the primary diagnostic question of the 48-Hour Awareness Inventory?",options:["'Did I explain my point logically enough?'","'What did the other person most likely experience from me?'","'Who was technically right in that disagreement?'","'How can I finish this meeting faster next time?'"],correct:1,explanation:"Asking 'What did they experience from me?' shifts attention from defending your intent to observing your actual interpersonal impact."}]},{id:"b01-l02",num:"02",title:"Emotional Signals at Work",duration:"22 min",type:"video",description:"Emotions are data, not drama. This module teaches you to read your own emotional signals accurately — before they turn into reactive behavior.",objectives:["Identify the four primary emotion families (Fear, Anger, Sadness, Shame) and the workplace needs they protect","Trace the Body-Emotion-Behavior chain to intercept reactivity at the somatic stage","Develop a high-granularity emotional vocabulary that reduces amygdala activation through affect labeling"],content:["Emotions are not interruptions to professional performance—they are your nervous system's real-time intelligence briefing on your environment. They are data, not drama.","The four primary emotion families each deliver specific organizational intelligence: Fear signals a perceived threat to safety or certainty; Anger signals a crossed boundary or blocked goal; Sadness signals loss of something valued; Shame signals a perceived threat to belonging or status.","Every emotional response follows the Body-Emotion-Behavior chain: a physiological shift occurs first (tight chest, heated face, shallow breath), followed by an emotional feeling, which then drives an impulse to act. Most professionals only notice the chain after the behavior has already slipped out.","Research by Dr. Matthew Lieberman at UCLA demonstrates that 'affect labeling'—putting a precise name to an emotion ('I am feeling sidelined and anxious about project ownership' rather than just 'I'm annoyed')—immediately activates the right ventrolateral prefrontal cortex and dampens amygdala reactivity."],tool:"Emotion Signal Map",lab:{title:"The Emotion Intercept",subtitle:"Decoding Data Before Drama",scenario:"During a project update meeting, a peer takes credit for a workflow solution your team built. You feel an immediate tightening in your chest and an impulse to interrupt sharply in front of leadership.",prompt:"Using the 3-step Emotion Intercept: (1) Name the exact emotion beneath the irritation with high granularity, (2) Identify the professional value or boundary it is protecting, and (3) Script the calm, authoritative response you will deliver instead of a reactive interruption.",accentColor:i.orange},quiz:[{q:"In the Vibe Hyr workplace framework, emotions are best understood as:",options:["Personal weaknesses that should be suppressed during working hours","Real-time neurological data signaling threats to boundaries, values, or safety","Unpredictable distractions that interfere with logical decision-making","Social performances designed to influence colleagues"],correct:1,explanation:"Emotions are electrochemical assessments generated by the limbic system to alert you to important environmental conditions."},{q:"Why does 'affect labeling' (naming an emotion with high specificity) reduce workplace reactivity?",options:["It distracts you from the person who upset you","It recruits the prefrontal cortex, which directly down-regulates amygdala firing","It proves to your colleagues that you have a large vocabulary","It suppresses the emotion so you never feel it again"],correct:1,explanation:"Neuroimaging studies confirm that precise affect labeling activates the prefrontal cortex and acts as a biological circuit breaker on amygdala activation."},{q:"What happens when a professional chronically suppresses or ignores early emotional signals at work?",options:["The signal disappears permanently and productivity increases","The signal amplifies in volume until it leaks out as disproportionate behavior or burnout","The amygdala stops monitoring the workplace for threats","Colleagues automatically assume the professional is highly regulated"],correct:1,explanation:"Suppressed emotional data does not vanish; it accumulates physiological tension until it erupts as regrettable behavior, passive-aggressiveness, or exhaustion."}]},{id:"b01-l03",num:"03",title:"Communication Architecture",duration:"25 min",type:"workshop",description:"Aligning Content, Context, and Subtext so your message lands without collateral friction.",objectives:["Apply the three-layer communication model (Content, Context, Subtext) to diagnose workplace misunderstandings","Distinguish between passive, aggressive, passive-aggressive, and grounded assertive communication","Master the Clarity Frame (Observation — Impact — Request) for high-stakes feedback and alignment"],content:["Every workplace communication operates on three simultaneous layers: Content (the explicit words and data), Context (the timing, medium, relationship history, and organizational climate), and Subtext (the emotional frequency and unspoken relational assumption beneath the delivery).","When Content and Subtext contradict each other, the human nervous system always believes the Subtext. Saying 'I'm totally open to feedback' with crossed arms, clipped tone, and defensive posture broadcasts a subtext of threat—and your team will respond to the subtext, not the words.","In written communication (Slack, email, project comments), vocal prosody and facial warmth are stripped away. Under workplace pressure, the human brain has a negativity bias that projects threat onto ambiguous text—turning a brief 'Let's discuss this' into a perceived reprimand.","The Clarity Frame aligns all three layers into a clean, non-threatening structure: (1) Observation — state the verifiable fact without character judgment; (2) Impact — name the concrete consequence on the work or team; (3) Request — specify the exact forward action needed."],tool:"Clarity Frame Worksheet",lab:{title:"The Clarity Frame Builder",subtitle:"Observation \xb7 Impact \xb7 Request",scenario:"A cross-functional partner has missed two consecutive handoff deadlines without advance notice, forcing your team to work late to hit the client delivery.",prompt:"Draft a 3-sentence message using the Clarity Frame (1: Verifiable Observation without blame, 2: Concrete Impact on the project/team, 3: Clear, collaborative Request for the next milestone) while keeping the Subtext calm and partnership-oriented.",accentColor:i.orange},quiz:[{q:"What are the three simultaneous layers of every professional communication?",options:["Verbal, nonverbal, and written channels","Content (what is said), Context (why/when/where), and Subtext (the emotional frequency beneath it)","Introduction, body paragraphs, and call-to-action","Sender, message medium, and receiver"],correct:1,explanation:"Every message carries explicit Content, sits inside a relational/situational Context, and transmits an implicit emotional Subtext."},{q:"When a leader's explicit Content ('My door is always open') contradicts their Subtext (impatient sighing, checking their phone while someone speaks), how do employees' nervous systems respond?",options:["They trust the explicit Content because it is official policy","They trust the Subtext, because the social nervous system prioritizes physiological threat/safety cues over words","They average the two signals and feel neutral","They ignore both and ask HR for clarification"],correct:1,explanation:"Neuroception reads prosody, posture, and micro-expressions faster than semantic language; when words and subtext conflict, the body always trusts the subtext."},{q:"Which sequence correctly represents the Vibe Hyr Clarity Frame?",options:["Compliment — Criticism — Compliment (the feedback sandwich)","Observation (verifiable fact) — Impact (concrete effect) — Request (specific forward action)","Emotion — Accusation — Consequence","Question — Debate — Directive"],correct:1,explanation:"Observation — Impact — Request keeps communication anchored to facts, clarifies stakes without personal attack, and creates forward momentum."}]},{id:"b01-l04",num:"04",title:"Trigger Mapping",duration:"20 min",type:"workshop",description:"Know your triggers before they run you. Map the 200-millisecond amygdala shortcut across the SCARF threat domains.",objectives:["Explain how a workplace trigger hijacks the prefrontal cortex in under 200 milliseconds","Categorize your top professional triggers using David Rock's SCARF model (Status, Certainty, Autonomy, Relatedness, Fairness)","Construct a personal Trigger Map linking specific stimuli to your earliest somatic warning cues"],content:["A trigger is not a conscious choice—it is a rapid threat signal sent to your amygdala that initiates a defensive cascade in under 200 milliseconds, long before your rational prefrontal cortex has evaluated what actually happened.","In the workplace, the amygdala treats social and professional threats with the same neural urgency as physical danger. Neuroleadership researcher David Rock identified five primary domains that trigger threat responses at work—the SCARF model: Status (feeling belittled or outranked), Certainty (ambiguity or shifting expectations), Autonomy (feeling micromanaged or boxed in), Relatedness (feeling excluded or unsafe in the group), and Fairness (perceiving double standards or inequitable credit).","When an unmapped trigger fires, you don't experience 'having a trigger'—you experience the situation as an objective emergency that justifies your fight (arguing, snapping), flight (avoiding, procrastinating), or freeze (shutting down in meetings) response.","By mapping your top recurring SCARF triggers and identifying the exact somatic signal (jaw clench, chest heat, shallow breath) that fires in the first two seconds, you move the trigger from an unconscious blind spot into an observable event you can pause and navigate."],tool:"Personal Trigger Map",lab:{title:"SCARF Trigger Archaeology",subtitle:"Mapping the 200ms Shortcut",scenario:"Your manager unexpectedly jumps into a project thread you own and starts reassigning subtasks in front of the team without asking you first. You feel an immediate surge of heat and defensiveness.",prompt:"Identify which of the 5 SCARF domains (Status, Certainty, Autonomy, Relatedness, Fairness) were just activated, name your default fight/flight/freeze impulse, and write the 60-second regulated response that protects your ownership without escalating tension.",accentColor:i.orange},quiz:[{q:"How quickly does the amygdala initiate a threat response when a workplace trigger fires?",options:["Approximately 3 to 5 seconds after conscious reflection","In under 200 milliseconds—before the prefrontal cortex can consciously evaluate the stimulus","After 1 to 2 minutes of conversation","Only after you decide to feel upset"],correct:1,explanation:"Subcortical threat detection fires in under 200ms, which is why physical sensations of activation appear before conscious rational analysis."},{q:"In the SCARF model of workplace triggers, what do the five letters stand for?",options:["Speed, Clarity, Action, Results, Feedback","Status, Certainty, Autonomy, Relatedness, Fairness","Safety, Calm, Authority, Respect, Focus","Structure, Communication, Accountability, Resilience, Frequency"],correct:1,explanation:"David Rock's SCARF framework identifies Status, Certainty, Autonomy, Relatedness, and Fairness as the five core social domains the brain monitors for threat or reward."},{q:"What is the primary purpose of building a Personal Trigger Map?",options:["To give colleagues a list of topics they are never allowed to bring up around you","To move recurring triggers from unconscious automatic reflexes into conscious observation so you can insert a pause","To eliminate all emotional reactions permanently","To justify why certain workplace behaviors make you angry"],correct:1,explanation:"A trigger that is mapped and recognized at its earliest physical signal can be paused and navigated; an unmapped trigger runs you automatically."}]},{id:"b01-l05",num:"05",title:"Vibe Hygiene",duration:"18 min",type:"video",description:"Your nervous system is contagious. Build a daily physiological stack that broadcasts regulation instead of leaking stress.",objectives:["Explain the neurobiology of workplace co-regulation and mirror neuron contagion","Design a 3-window Vibe Hygiene Daily Stack (Pre-Work, Pre-Meeting Reset, Evening Revision)","Use the Physiological Sigh and Evening Revision to prevent cortisol carryover between work and home"],content:["Your nervous system is not private. Through mirror neurons and autonomic co-regulation, the physiological state you walk into a room or Zoom call with becomes a biological input for everyone present.","When you walk into a meeting carrying unprocessed residue from your previous stressful call—elevated heart rate, rapid speech, tense facial muscles—your colleagues' threat-detection systems register that signal within seconds. Leaking chronic stress creates a low-grade threat environment that degrades team creativity and psychological safety.","Vibe Hygiene is the daily discipline of cleaning and resetting your nervous system state before, during, and after high-demand professional interactions. It relies on direct physiological levers rather than willpower: the Physiological Sigh (two quick inhales through the nose followed by one extended exhale through the mouth) rapidly offloads carbon dioxide and stimulates the vagus nerve to slow heart rate in under 60 seconds.","Your Vibe Hygiene Stack operates across three daily windows: (1) Morning Baseline Set before opening email, (2) 90-second Transitions between back-to-back meetings so you never carry old conflict into a new room, and (3) Evening Revision at the close of the workday to reconsolidate any friction and leave work energy at work."],tool:"Vibe Hygiene Daily Stack",lab:{title:"The 90-Second Transition Reset",subtitle:"Stopping Emotional Carryover",scenario:"You just wrapped a tense, frustrating 30-minute call where a budget or timeline was cut. You have 90 seconds before joining a creative brainstorming session with your core team.",prompt:"Design your exact 90-second Vibe Hygiene reset between these two meetings: what physiological breath/posture cue will you execute, what internal phrase will close the previous call's loop, and what energy intention will you set before clicking 'Join'?",accentColor:i.orange},quiz:[{q:"Why is poor 'Vibe Hygiene' in a leader or teammate harmful to the entire group?",options:["It violates corporate dress and etiquette codes","Mirror neurons and autonomic co-regulation cause leaked stress to trigger low-grade threat responses across the team","It causes meetings to run over their scheduled time","It only affects the individual who is stressed and has no impact on others"],correct:1,explanation:"Human nervous systems synchronize via mirror neurons and neuroception; leaked sympathetic activation broadcasts threat and suppresses collective prefrontal performance."},{q:"How is the 'Physiological Sigh' performed for rapid autonomic down-regulation?",options:["Holding your breath for 20 seconds as hard as you can","Rapid, shallow chest breathing for 60 seconds","Two inhales through the nose (a full inhale topped off by a second sharp sip of air) followed by a long, slow exhale through the mouth","Breathing normally while thinking about your to-do list"],correct:2,explanation:"The double inhale reinflates collapsed alveoli in the lungs, and the extended exhale activates the vagus nerve to rapidly lower heart rate."},{q:"What are the three daily windows of the Vibe Hygiene Stack?",options:["Monday morning, Wednesday noon, Friday afternoon","Pre-Work Baseline Set, Pre-Meeting 90-Second Transition Reset, and Evening Revision/Transition Ritual","Quarterly review, annual review, and exit interview","Coffee break, lunch break, and commute"],correct:1,explanation:"Setting your baseline before work, clearing residue between meetings, and completing an Evening Revision ritual prevents cumulative dysregulation."}]},{id:"b01-l06",num:"06",title:"Live Integration Session",duration:"60 min",type:"live",isLive:!0,description:"Facilitated group session and capstone integration for Track 01: Common Sense in the Workplace.",objectives:["Synthesize all five Track 01 tools into real-world workplace case studies","Practice the Clarity Frame and Emotion Intercept with live prosody and subtext calibration","Lock in your 30-day Track 01 Integration Commitment across awareness, communication, and vibe hygiene"],content:["This capstone module integrates all five Track 01 pillars—The Awareness Gap, Emotional Signals at Work, Communication Architecture, Trigger Mapping, and Vibe Hygiene—into an executable daily operating standard.","In the live facilitated session, you will work through realistic workplace friction scenarios in small groups, testing how your Clarity Frame scripts (Observation — Impact — Request) land on other nervous systems when time pressure and competing priorities are present.","To prepare for the session (or to complete your self-guided capstone review today), bring your top Awareness Gap discovery from Module 01, your SCARF Trigger Map from Module 04, and one active workplace conversation that requires a Clarity Frame intervention.","Complete the Track 01 Integration Lab and Capstone Check below to formalize your 30-day workplace commitment and unlock your Track 01 completion credit."],tool:"Track 01 Integration Commitment",lab:{title:"Track 01 Capstone Commitment",subtitle:"30-Day Workplace Operating Standard",scenario:"You are stepping into the next 30 days of your work life equipped with the Awareness Gap audit, the Emotion Intercept, the Clarity Frame, your SCARF Trigger Map, and your Vibe Hygiene Stack.",prompt:"Write your 3-part Track 01 Commitment: (1) The #1 Awareness Gap or SCARF trigger I am actively closing this month, (2) The exact Clarity Frame conversation I will initiate this week, and (3) My non-negotiable daily Vibe Hygiene anchor.",accentColor:i.orange},quiz:[{q:"When integrating Track 01 in a real workplace scenario, what is the correct sequence when you feel a sudden surge of frustration during a meeting?",options:["Speak immediately so people know how strongly you feel, then apologize later","Notice the somatic trigger (<200ms), apply a Physiological Sigh + Affect Label, check your Subtext, and respond using the Clarity Frame","Suppress the feeling completely and complain to a peer after the meeting","Leave the meeting without explanation"],correct:1,explanation:"Track 01 integrates somatic trigger recognition, physiological regulation, emotional granularity, and structured Clarity Frame communication."},{q:"Which combination best closes the Awareness Gap during a high-stakes feedback conversation?",options:["Focusing 100% on your good intentions and ignoring how the other person reacts","Regulating your nervous system before entering (Vibe Hygiene), aligning warm/steady Subtext with factual Content (Clarity Frame), and asking 'What did they experience from me?'","Sending a blunt late-night email so you don't have to see their facial expression","Using generalizations like 'You always do this' to make the point memorable"],correct:1,explanation:"Closing the Awareness Gap requires pre-interaction regulation, aligned Content/Context/Subtext, and outcome-focused observation of impact."},{q:"Why does the Evening Revision ritual belong in a professional Vibe Hygiene Stack?",options:["It forces you to work extra hours every night","It reconsolidates the day's friction from a regulated perspective and signals to your amygdala that the workday threat context has ended","It helps you memorize your calendar for tomorrow","It replaces sleep with visualization"],correct:1,explanation:"Evening Revision neutralizes lingering emotional charge via memory reconsolidation and prevents cortisol carryover into your personal life."}]}]},{id:"from-reaction-to-response",num:"02",tag:"De-Escalation",title:"From Reaction to Response",subtitle:"The Escalation Ladder \xb7 Stage Intervention \xb7 Pattern Interrupts",color:i.gold,totalTime:"~2.5 hrs",description:"Conflict isn't the problem — unconscious escalation is. This track installs a five-stage Escalation Ladder, early Stage 1 intervention scripts, pattern interrupts, and the PAUSE response protocol.",lessons:[{id:"b02-l01",num:"01",title:"The Reactivity Spectrum",duration:"18 min",type:"video",description:"Why smart professionals do things they immediately regret — and how to locate your exact threshold before the point of no return.",objectives:["Map the neurological continuum from baseline ventral-vagal calm to full amygdala hijack","Identify the progressive order in which cognitive faculties (empathy, nuance, self-monitoring) go offline as cortisol rises","Pinpoint your personal 'point of no return' threshold and its earliest somatic warning markers"],content:["The Reactivity Spectrum runs from baseline calm (Level 1) to full amygdala hijack (Level 5). Every professional moves along this spectrum daily; mastery is knowing your exact coordinate in real time before you cross the point of no return.","As perceived threat increases and stress hormones flood the bloodstream, cognitive faculties do not disappear all at once—they drop offline in a predictable sequence. The first casualty is genuine empathy (curiosity about the other person's view). The second casualty is cognitive nuance (gray-area thinking collapses into binary right/wrong). The third casualty is self-monitoring—at which point you are in full amygdala hijack.","Once in a full Level 4–5 amygdala hijack, your prefrontal cortex is functionally offline. Attempting to 'reason your way through' or resolve a complex disagreement in this state is neurobiologically futile; only physiological down-regulation can restore prefrontal access.","Every professional has a distinct reactivity signature: some escalate rapidly into vocal sharpness, while others escalate into icy withdrawal or rigid over-control. Mapping your personal signature turns unconscious reactivity into an early-warning dashboard."],tool:"Reactivity Spectrum Self-Assessment",lab:{title:"Reactivity Spectrum Calibration",subtitle:"The Sovereign Interrupt",scenario:"An accusatory email from a client or senior stakeholder lands in your inbox copying your leadership team, blaming you for a delay caused by their own side. You feel your face heat up, your jaw tighten, and your fingers start typing a scorching reply.",prompt:"Rate your current coordinate on the Reactivity Spectrum (1–5), name which cognitive faculties (empathy, nuance, self-monitoring) are already compromised, and write the exact physiological + behavioral sequence you will execute before touching the keyboard.",accentColor:i.gold},quiz:[{q:"Once a professional is in a full Level 5 amygdala hijack, which intervention is neurobiologically viable first?",options:["Complex logical debate to prove who is right","Deep empathetic perspective-taking","Physiological regulation (breath, pause, physical recess) to restore prefrontal cortex blood flow","Pushing through to a final binding decision immediately"],correct:2,explanation:"During an amygdala hijack, the prefrontal cortex is offline. Physiological regulation is the only available doorway back to cognitive reasoning and empathy."},{q:"As a professional moves up the Reactivity Spectrum under stress, which cognitive capacity is typically the FIRST casualty to go offline?",options:["Basic motor control and speech","Genuine empathy and curiosity about the other person's perspective","Memory of your own job title","The ability to feel anger"],correct:1,explanation:"Empathy and perspective-taking require the highest prefrontal metabolic resources and are the first faculties lost when threat arousal rises."},{q:"What is the strategic purpose of identifying your personal 'Reactivity Signature'?",options:["To excuse reactive behavior by saying 'that's just my personality'","To recognize your earliest internal warning signals at Level 2 so you can intervene before reaching the point of no return","To diagnose your colleagues' flaws during arguments","To avoid ever having difficult conversations at work"],correct:1,explanation:"Knowing your early physical and cognitive markers at Level 2 gives you an intervention window while your prefrontal cortex is still online."}]},{id:"b02-l02",num:"02",title:"The Escalation Ladder",duration:"22 min",type:"workshop",description:"A five-stage conflict map — from Stage 1 Friction to Stage 5 Rupture — and the exact intervention window for each rung.",objectives:["Identify the behavioral and neurological markers of all five stages: Friction, Tension, Conflict, Crisis, and Rupture","Diagnose where an active workplace disagreement sits on the Escalation Ladder before speaking","Match the right intervention strategy to each stage so you never apply a Stage 1 tool to a Stage 4 crisis"],content:["Workplace conflict never arrives at Stage 5 out of nowhere—it climbs a predictable five-rung Escalation Ladder: Stage 1 (Friction), Stage 2 (Tension), Stage 3 (Conflict), Stage 4 (Crisis), and Stage 5 (Rupture).","At Stage 1 (Friction), there is a minor misalignment or awkward moment, but no negative story about the other person's character has formed yet. By Stage 2 (Tension), the friction went unaddressed and a silent narrative begins accumulating ('They always ignore our input').","At Stage 3 (Conflict), positions harden into 'me vs. you,' intent is negatively attributed, and reasoning is used to defend ego rather than solve the problem. At Stage 4 (Crisis), emotional flooding takes over—voices tighten or shut down, and the goal shifts to winning or escaping. At Stage 5 (Rupture), trust breaks down completely, requiring formal repair or mediation.","The cardinal rule of de-escalation: match your move to the rung. Stage 1 and 2 respond to direct curiosity and clarification. Stage 3 requires a pattern reset—naming the dynamic rather than debating the content. Stage 4 requires an immediate pause/recess protocol because neither brain can process solutions while flooded."],tool:"Escalation Ladder Reference Card",lab:{title:"Escalation Ladder Diagnostic",subtitle:"Matching Move to Rung",scenario:"In a product planning meeting, two department leads stop discussing the data and start interrupting each other with phrases like 'If your team had actually read our spec last month...' and 'We're always cleaning up your rushed launches.'",prompt:"Identify which stage of the Escalation Ladder (1–5) this conversation has reached, explain why adding more technical data right now will fail, and write the exact sentence you will use to step in and reset the rung.",accentColor:i.gold},quiz:[{q:"What is the defining transition that moves a workplace dynamic from Stage 1 (Friction) to Stage 2 (Tension) and Stage 3 (Conflict)?",options:["More people are invited to the calendar meeting","Unaddressed friction accumulates a negative narrative and attribution of intent ('they are doing this on purpose')","A formal HR complaint is filed","The project deadline is extended"],correct:1,explanation:"At Stage 1, friction is just a moment of misalignment. When unaddressed, the brain constructs a defensive narrative about the other person's motives (Stage 2–3)."},{q:"When a conversation has escalated to Stage 3 (positional Conflict) or Stage 4 (emotional Crisis), what is the recommended intervention?",options:["Restate your logical arguments louder and more forcefully","Initiate a Pattern Reset or Pause/Recess protocol so both nervous systems can down-regulate before problem-solving","Force an immediate vote from everyone in the room","Pretend nothing is wrong and move to the next agenda item"],correct:1,explanation:"At Stage 3–4, prefrontal access is compromised. You must pause the positional debate or take a physiological recess before problem-solving can succeed."},{q:"Why does a Stage 1 clarification script fail when deployed in the middle of a Stage 4 Crisis?",options:["Stage 1 scripts are too polite for senior executives","At Stage 4, emotional flooding has taken the prefrontal cortex offline, so nuanced verbal clarification is perceived as further threat or manipulation","Stage 1 scripts only work in written emails","Stage 4 requires at least ten people in the room"],correct:1,explanation:"When parties are emotionally flooded at Stage 4, their brains cannot process cognitive clarification; space and physiological regulation must come first."}]},{id:"b02-l03",num:"03",title:"Stage 1 Intervention",duration:"20 min",type:"workshop",description:"The highest-leverage place to resolve conflict is Stage 1 — before stories harden and egos entrench.",objectives:["Master the four Stage 1 intervention moves: Acknowledge, Clarify, Redirect, and Release","Recognize and overcome the micro-avoidance habit that lets Stage 1 friction ferment into Stage 3 conflict","Deliver 30-second Stage 1 check-ins that preserve psychological safety and relationship equity"],content:["The most powerful de-escalators rarely have to perform dramatic Stage 4 rescues—because they systematically clear friction at Stage 1 while both parties still have 100% prefrontal access and zero ego entrenchment.","Most professionals skip Stage 1 intervention because of a false economy: 'It's just a small thing, I don't want to make it awkward.' In reality, avoiding 30 seconds of mild Stage 1 candor almost always purchases 3 weeks of Stage 3 resentment.","The Stage 1 Toolkit consists of four lightweight moves: (1) Acknowledge — name the micro-tension warmly ('I sensed my comment might have landed abruptly—let me check in'); (2) Clarify — close assumption gaps ('Can I check my understanding of what success looks like for Friday?'); (3) Redirect — move focus from person to process ('I think our workflow handoff is creating this bottleneck—let's look at the template'); (4) Release — let the other person feel fully heard so minor friction dissolves on the spot.","Because Stage 1 interventions happen while ventral-vagal safety is intact, they actually build trust rather than spending it. Every Stage 1 check-in deposits relational equity into the team bank."],tool:"Stage 1 Conversation Scripts",lab:{title:"The 30-Second Stage 1 Clear",subtitle:"Acknowledge \xb7 Clarify \xb7 Redirect \xb7 Release",scenario:"After a 1-on-1 meeting, you notice a normally engaged teammate gave unusually short answers and left without their usual warmth. Nothing explicitly confrontational was said, but you feel a distinct Stage 1 micro-friction.",prompt:"Instead of brushing it off and letting it ferment into Stage 2 tension, choose one of the four Stage 1 moves (Acknowledge, Clarify, Redirect, Release) and write the exact 2-sentence message or hallway check-in you will deliver today.",accentColor:i.gold},quiz:[{q:"Why is Stage 1 the highest-leverage intervention point on the entire Escalation Ladder?",options:["Because you can win the argument before the other person prepares their defense","Because both parties still have full prefrontal cortex access and no defensive narrative about intent has hardened yet","Because Stage 1 interventions can be delegated to HR","Because Stage 1 only happens once a year"],correct:1,explanation:"At Stage 1, empathy and cognitive flexibility are fully online and neither party has built an adversarial story to defend."},{q:"What are the four moves in the Vibe Hyr Stage 1 Intervention Toolkit?",options:["Confront, Debate, Escalate, Document","Acknowledge, Clarify, Redirect, Release","Ignore, Delay, Complain, Resign","Observe, Judge, Warn, Penalize"],correct:1,explanation:"Acknowledge, Clarify, Redirect, and Release resolve early friction rapidly while strengthening relational safety."},{q:"When friction is caused by an ambiguous workflow or handoff rather than interpersonal ill-will, which Stage 1 move shifts the conversation from 'person' to 'process'?",options:["Redirect ('I think the disconnect is in how our handoff template is structured—let's adjust the process')","Silent withdrawal","Formal escalation to executive leadership","Personal critique"],correct:0,explanation:"Redirect depersonalizes friction by pointing both people's attention toward fixing the shared system or process."}]},{id:"b02-l04",num:"04",title:"Pattern Interrupts",duration:"22 min",type:"workshop",description:"Recurring conflict loops are more dangerous than single events. Design physiological, cognitive, and behavioral interrupts that break the automated chain.",objectives:["Deconstruct recurring workplace conflict loops into Trigger → Sensation → Story → Behavior → Reinforcing Outcome","Design four types of Pattern Interrupts: Physiological, Cognitive, Behavioral, and Environmental","Pair your Pattern Interrupt with a Sovereign identity assumption so the old loop loses its RAS confirmation"],content:["A single workplace disagreement is an event. Having the exact same argument or shutdown dynamic with the same colleague every two weeks is a conditioned neural loop—and it cannot be solved by trying harder inside the same pattern.","Through Hebbian plasticity ('neurons that fire together, wire together'), recurring conflict sequences become automated: Stimulus (colleague's skeptical tone) → Sensation (chest tightness) → Story ('They don't respect my expertise') → Behavior (defensive over-explaining) → Outcome (colleague tunes out, confirming your story).","The goal of a Pattern Interrupt is not to change the other person's initial stimulus—it is to break the automated chain between your earliest physical sensation and your habitual behavior. When one link in a conditioned loop changes, the entire downstream dynamic is forced to reorganize.","Effective Pattern Interrupts can be Physiological (a slow 6-second exhale and physically sitting back in your chair), Cognitive (silently labeling: 'There is the Prover story running'), Behavioral (replacing defensive explanation with a single calm question: 'What part of this feels riskiest to you?'), or Environmental (switching from a heated Slack thread to a 3-minute walk-and-talk)."],tool:"Pattern Interrupt Design Template",lab:{title:"Loop Breaker Lab",subtitle:"Designing Your Pattern Interrupt",scenario:"Every time a specific stakeholder questions your timeline in a status meeting, you automatically launch into a rapid, defensive 3-minute justification of how hard your team is working—which only makes the stakeholder interrupt more aggressively.",prompt:"Map this loop and design a 3-part Pattern Interrupt (1: Physical/Postural shift the second they ask the question, 2: Internal identity reframe, 3: A 1-sentence calm response that breaks the defensive justification habit).",accentColor:i.gold},quiz:[{q:"In neuroscience and workplace dynamics, a recurring conflict pattern is maintained because:",options:["One person is naturally difficult and cannot learn","A repeating Trigger → Sensation → Story → Behavior → Outcome loop becomes myelinated and runs as an automatic reflex","Company policies require weekly debates","The calendar invites are scheduled at the wrong time of day"],correct:1,explanation:"Repeated interpersonal sequences wire together into an automatic reflex loop where each person's habitual reaction confirms the other's expectation."},{q:"Where in the conflict sequence must a Pattern Interrupt be inserted to be most effective?",options:["Two days after the argument is over","Between the earliest detectable physical sensation/trigger and your habitual behavioral response","Only after the other person apologizes first","During the annual performance review"],correct:1,explanation:"Catching the earliest somatic cue allows you to insert a physiological, cognitive, or behavioral interrupt before the old behavior executes."},{q:"Switching a tense back-and-forth Slack or email thread into a brief, calm phone call or walk-and-talk is an example of which type of Pattern Interrupt?",options:["An Environmental / Medium Pattern Interrupt","Avoidance behavior","Escalation to Stage 5","Passive compliance"],correct:0,explanation:"Changing the communication medium or physical environment disrupts the conditioned stimulus-response context and restores vocal prosody and human connection."}]},{id:"b02-l05",num:"05",title:"Response Protocols",duration:"20 min",type:"video",description:"Pre-built response frameworks — PAUSE, REFRAME, and EXIT — that execute reliably when pressure makes improvisation impossible.",objectives:["Execute the 5-step PAUSE Protocol (Physiological, Acknowledge, Understand, Select, Execute) in under 15 seconds","Deploy the REFRAME script to convert adversarial positioning into joint problem-solving","Use the dignity-preserving EXIT protocol when a conversation reaches Stage 4 flooding"],content:["In high-stakes moments, you do not rise to the level of your intentions—you fall to the level of your pre-built protocols. When cortisol spikes, working memory shrinks, making clever verbal improvisation almost impossible.","The master framework for real-time response is the PAUSE Protocol: (P) Physiological — drop shoulders and take one slow exhale before speaking; (A) Acknowledge — silently name your internal state ('I am feeling defensive'); (U) Understand — ask one genuine clarifying question before stating your defense; (S) Select — choose your intentional response move (Clarify, Reframe, or Exit); (E) Execute — speak at 80% of your normal speed with lowered vocal pitch.","The REFRAME Protocol shifts conversational geometry from face-to-face opposition to side-by-side problem-solving: 'It sounds like we are both trying to protect the launch quality, just looking at different risks. Help me see the risk from your side first.'","The EXIT Protocol is your circuit breaker when an interaction hits Stage 4 emotional flooding: 'I care too much about getting this right to rush it while we're both activated. Let's take 20 minutes to regroup and reconvene at 3:00 PM.' A clean EXIT specifies both the commitment to the outcome and the exact time of return, preventing abandonment threat."],tool:"Response Protocol Quick-Reference Card",lab:{title:"The PAUSE & EXIT Simulator",subtitle:"High-Pressure Protocol Execution",scenario:"A colleague raises their voice in a hybrid meeting and says, 'Your team's bottleneck is the only reason we're missing our quarterly target, and everyone on this call knows it!' The room goes dead silent.",prompt:"Walk through your exact 5-step PAUSE sequence in this moment, and write both (A) a REFRAME response if they are at Stage 3, and (B) a dignity-preserving EXIT response if they continue at Stage 4.",accentColor:i.gold},quiz:[{q:"In the 5-step PAUSE Protocol, what do the letters P-A-U-S-E stand for?",options:["Plan, Argue, Win, Summarize, Exit","Physiological regulation, Acknowledge internal state, Understand the other's position, Select a response protocol, Execute deliberately","Postpone, Avoid, Unsubscribe, Silence, Escape","Prepare, Announce, Update, Schedule, Evaluate"],correct:1,explanation:"PAUSE sequences body regulation first, internal awareness second, curiosity third, deliberate selection fourth, and slow prosodic execution fifth."},{q:"Why does the Execute step of PAUSE specify speaking at roughly 80% of your normal pace with a slightly lowered vocal pitch?",options:["To make the meeting last longer","Because slow cadence and lower prosody broadcast a ventral-vagal safety signal to every nervous system in the room while keeping your prefrontal cortex online","To sound like a radio announcer","Because people cannot hear high-pitched voices on Zoom"],correct:1,explanation:"Slowing your vocal cadence by 20% overrides sympathetic urgency and actively co-regulates both your own brainstem and the listener's."},{q:"What two elements must every dignity-preserving EXIT protocol statement include so it doesn't trigger abandonment or stonewalling threat?",options:["An apology and a resignation letter","Explicit commitment to the shared outcome/relationship AND a specific time to reconvene once regulated","A list of the other person's mistakes and a warning","Silence and walking out of the room"],correct:1,explanation:"Walking out without naming your commitment and a specific return time feels like stonewalling (which spikes threat); naming both creates a safe container to cool down."}]},{id:"b02-l06",num:"06",title:"Live De-Escalation Workshop",duration:"60 min",type:"live",isLive:!0,description:"Live facilitated roleplay workshop and capstone integration for Track 02: From Reaction to Response.",objectives:["Practice Stage 1–4 de-escalation scripts under simulated workplace pressure","Receive live prosody and somatic calibration feedback from facilitator and peers","Finalize your Personal De-Escalation Protocol Card for daily professional deployment"],content:["This live workshop and capstone module brings all five Track 02 de-escalation systems—The Reactivity Spectrum, The Escalation Ladder, Stage 1 Interventions, Pattern Interrupts, and the PAUSE/REFRAME/EXIT Response Protocols—into live muscle memory.","Using structured triad roleplays (Initiator, Responder, Somatic Observer), you will practice catching your earliest Level 2 physical cue, firing your custom Pattern Interrupt, and delivering a slow-prosody REFRAME or EXIT under realistic pushback.","Before attending the live workshop (or as your self-guided capstone today), complete your Personal De-Escalation Protocol Card in the Lab below so your exact scripts are written, rehearsed aloud, and ready for immediate workplace deployment.","Pass the Track 02 Capstone Check below to complete From Reaction to Response and unlock your Track 02 certification credit."],tool:"Personal De-Escalation Protocol Card",lab:{title:"Personal De-Escalation Protocol Card",subtitle:"Track 02 Capstone Blueprint",scenario:"You are building your permanent pocket reference card for high-stakes workplace friction so you never have to improvise while cortisol is elevated.",prompt:"Write your 4-part Protocol Card: (1) My earliest Level 2 somatic cue, (2) My chosen 10-second Pattern Interrupt, (3) My verbatim Stage 1 / REFRAME script, and (4) My verbatim Stage 4 EXIT script.",accentColor:i.gold},quiz:[{q:"Across the entire Track 02 de-escalation system, what is the single biggest mistake professionals make during workplace conflict?",options:["Intervening too early at Stage 1 with curiosity","Waiting until Stage 3 or 4 to address friction, and then trying to use logical debate while both brains are in sympathetic threat arousal","Taking a 6-second exhale before speaking","Asking clarifying questions"],correct:1,explanation:"Avoiding early Stage 1 friction and then trying to debate logically during Stage 3–4 amygdala activation is the root cause of workplace conflict spirals."},{q:"When you notice yourself at Level 3 on the Reactivity Spectrum during a tense meeting, what is the immediate first step of the PAUSE protocol?",options:["Interrupt the speaker to correct their facts","Physiological regulation—drop your shoulders, soften your jaw, and take a slow exhale before saying a word","Send a private message to a colleague complaining about the meeting","Leave the company"],correct:1,explanation:"Physiological regulation (P) always comes first because you cannot access cognitive perspective-taking while your autonomic nervous system is mobilizing for fight-or-flight."},{q:"How does a pre-scripted REFRAME statement shift the neurobiology of a Stage 2–3 disagreement?",options:["It tricks the other person into agreeing with everything you say","It reduces social threat by naming a shared goal and inviting their perspective first, allowing their amygdala to stand down","It proves you know more jargon than they do","It ends the meeting immediately without solving the issue"],correct:1,explanation:"Naming shared intent and inviting the other person's view removes Status and Relatedness threat, bringing their prefrontal cortex back into collaborative problem-solving."}]}]},{id:"know-yourself-lead-yourself",num:"03",tag:"Self Mastery",title:"Know Yourself, Lead Yourself",subtitle:"Identity Architecture \xb7 Assumption Audit \xb7 90-Day Shift Plan",color:i.purple,totalTime:"~3 hrs",description:"Lasting leadership begins with self-concept. This track combines cognitive neuroscience and the Law of Assumption to audit limiting professional beliefs and install a Sovereign 90-day identity shift.",lessons:[{id:"b03-l01",num:"01",title:"Identity Architecture",duration:"22 min",type:"video",description:"Professional behavior is downstream of self-concept. Map the three layers of identity that program your workplace RAS.",objectives:["Explain how your Reticular Activating System (RAS) uses your assumed professional identity as its primary filtering instruction","Map the three nested layers of professional performance: Surface (Behavior), Structural (Rules/Beliefs), and Architectural (Core Identity Assumption)","Locate the exact gap between your current default workplace self-concept and your target leadership identity"],content:["Professional behavior is never purely a function of skill—it is downstream of identity. You do not rise to the level of your career goals; you fall to the level of your habitual assumptions about who you are at work.","Your Reticular Activating System (RAS) filters 11 million bits of sensory data per second down to roughly 40–50 bits of conscious awareness. Its primary sorting criterion is your assumed identity. If you privately assume 'I am someone who gets overlooked in senior rooms,' your RAS will spotlight every micro-moment of being interrupted and filter out opportunities to claim authority.","Professional identity operates across three nested layers: (1) The Surface Layer — your visible habits, communication patterns, and execution; (2) The Structural Layer — the unspoken rules and beliefs governing what feels safe or allowed; and (3) The Architectural Layer — the root 'I am...' assumption at the center of your Default Mode Network.","Most corporate training fails because it tries to glue new Surface behaviors onto an unchanged Architectural assumption. When pressure hits, the Surface tactic snaps back to match the root identity. True self-mastery rewires the Architecture first."],tool:"Identity Architecture Map",lab:{title:"Three-Layer Identity Audit",subtitle:"Surface \xb7 Structural \xb7 Architectural",scenario:"Think of one recurring professional limitation you have tried to fix through willpower alone (e.g., hesitating to speak up in executive meetings, overworking to avoid criticism, or holding back on pitching bold ideas).",prompt:"Trace that pattern down all three layers: (1) Surface Behavior (what you visibly do), (2) Structural Rule ('If I do X, then Y bad thing will happen'), and (3) The root Architectural Identity Assumption — then write the new Architectural 'I am...' assumption that replaces it.",accentColor:i.purple},quiz:[{q:"In the Vibe Hyr Self-Mastery framework, professional identity is defined as:",options:["A fixed set of personality traits determined at birth","Your habitual, deeply held assumption about who you are—which instructs your RAS on how to filter workplace reality","The official job description written by HR","The sum total of your past mistakes"],correct:1,explanation:"Identity is an operational assumption stored in the Default Mode Network that directs the RAS filter and governs automatic behavior under pressure."},{q:"What are the three nested layers of the Identity Architecture model?",options:["Junior, Mid-level, and Senior","Surface Layer (behaviors/habits), Structural Layer (rules/beliefs), and Architectural Layer (core identity assumption)","Morning, Afternoon, and Evening","Physical, Financial, and Social"],correct:1,explanation:"Surface behaviors are driven by Structural rules/beliefs, which are organized around the root Architectural identity assumption."},{q:"Why do surface-level behavioral tactics usually collapse when a professional encounters high workplace stress?",options:["Because behavioral tactics are too complicated to remember","Because under stress, the nervous system defaults to whatever matches the root Architectural identity assumption in the Default Mode Network","Because colleagues don't like new behaviors","Because tactics only work for executives"],correct:1,explanation:"Unless the root Architectural assumption is updated, cognitive load and stress cause the brain to revert to its most myelinated default identity."}]},{id:"b03-l02",num:"02",title:"The Four Archetypes",duration:"25 min",type:"workshop",description:"Recognize the four professional operating modes — Performer, Protector, Prover, and Sovereign — and choose who is driving.",objectives:["Identify the core assumption, strengths, and hidden costs of the Performer, Protector, and Prover archetypes","Define the Sovereign archetype and its neurobiological foundation of intrinsic, non-contingent worth","Diagnose which archetype hijacks your decision-making in specific high-pressure workplace contexts"],content:["Under professional pressure, most people cycle through four distinct identity archetypes: three defensive survival modes (Performer, Protector, Prover) and one grounded leadership mode (Sovereign).","The Performer operates from the assumption: 'I am my results and applause.' They deliver high output but suffer from chronic perfectionism and terror of visible failure. The Protector operates from: 'Safety means not standing out or making waves.' They are diplomatic and agreeable on the surface, but chronically under-claim their value and swallow frustration until they burn out.","The Prover operates from: 'I must constantly outwork everyone to prove I am never inadequate.' They hustle relentlessly, struggle to delegate, deflect praise, and treat every project as a referendum on their worth.","The Sovereign operates from a fundamentally different assumption: 'My value is intrinsic and non-contingent; I lead and contribute from wholeness, not from a need to prove or hide.' The goal is not to judge your Performer, Protector, or Prover—it is to notice when one has grabbed the steering wheel and consciously shift back into Sovereign authority."],tool:"Archetype Recognition Journal",lab:{title:"Archetype Steering Wheel Check",subtitle:"Performer \xb7 Protector \xb7 Prover → Sovereign",scenario:"You are preparing for a high-visibility presentation or negotiation where senior decision-makers will scrutinize your numbers and push back on your recommendations.",prompt:"Identify which defensive archetype (Performer, Protector, or Prover) typically takes the wheel for you in this scenario, describe the hidden cost it exacts on your presence, and write the 3-sentence Sovereign state induction you will use 90 seconds before entering the room.",accentColor:i.purple},quiz:[{q:"What is the core operating assumption of the Sovereign professional archetype?",options:["'I am only as valuable as my most recent quarterly metric'","'Safety comes from staying invisible and avoiding all disagreement'","'I must out-hustle everyone so no one ever questions my competence'","'My professional value is intrinsic and non-contingent; I contribute and lead from grounded authority rather than fear or need'"],correct:3,explanation:"Unlike the Performer, Protector, and Prover (whose worth is contingent on applause, invisibility, or endless hustle), the Sovereign leads from intrinsic grounded worth."},{q:"A talented manager who refuses to delegate, works 70 hours a week, and feels anxious whenever they aren't visibly fixing a problem is primarily operating from which archetype?",options:["The Protector","The Prover","The Sovereign","The Observer"],correct:1,explanation:"The Prover assumes their worth must be constantly re-earned through relentless effort and over-functioning, making delegation and rest feel unsafe."},{q:"What is the healthiest way to work with the Four Archetypes in daily professional life?",options:["Feel ashamed whenever you notice Performer, Protector, or Prover tendencies","Recognize which archetype is currently driving in a given context without judgment, and consciously choose to shift into Sovereign presence","Force everyone on your team to take a personality test and label them permanently","Eliminate all ambition and stop caring about results"],correct:1,explanation:"Archetypes are operational states, not permanent prison sentences. Noticing which one is driving gives you the choice to shift into Sovereign authority."}]},{id:"b03-l03",num:"03",title:"The Assumption Audit",duration:"22 min",type:"workshop",description:"Surface the unconscious assumptions holding your career ceiling in place — and build a competing RAS evidence file.",objectives:["Distinguish between a conscious opinion and an unconscious limiting assumption that programs the RAS","Apply the reverse-engineering diagnostic question: 'What must I assume to be true about myself for this behavior to make sense?'","Execute Assumption Disruption by building a daily counter-evidence archive for your RAS"],content:["An assumption is not a casual opinion you hold—it is an operational belief that holds you. Because assumptions live in the subconscious Default Mode Network, they don't feel like beliefs; they feel like 'just the way things are.'","While a conscious thought merely passes through awareness, a root assumption acts as a standing order to your Reticular Activating System (RAS). If you carry the assumption 'Senior leadership doesn't listen to people at my level,' your RAS will highlight every dismissed comment as proof and discard every invitation to contribute as a fluke.","To surface an invisible assumption, never ask 'What are my limiting beliefs?'—your analytical mind will give you a polished, socially acceptable answer. Instead, audit your recurring unwanted behaviors and ask: 'What must I privately assume to be true about myself or this workplace for that behavior to make complete logical sense?'","Once an assumption is surfaced on paper, it loses its invisibility. You disrupt its neural monopoly not by chanting hollow affirmations, but by giving your RAS a competing assignment: systematically collecting 3 pieces of real-world counter-evidence every single day."],tool:"Professional Assumption Audit",lab:{title:"Professional Assumption Audit",subtitle:"Reverse-Engineering the Invisible Ceiling",scenario:"Look at an area of your career where your progress, influence, or compensation has plateaued despite your strong technical capability.",prompt:"Answer the diagnostic question honestly: 'What must I currently assume to be true about myself, my value, or how this organization works for my current habits around visibility and authority to make sense?' Then list 3 real pieces of counter-evidence from your career that prove the opposite assumption is already viable.",accentColor:i.purple},quiz:[{q:"How does a deep professional assumption differ from a casual conscious opinion?",options:["An assumption is always false, whereas an opinion is always true","An assumption operates below conscious awareness as a standing instruction to the RAS, selectively filtering reality to confirm itself","An assumption only applies outside of work","There is no difference between the two"],correct:1,explanation:"Assumptions act as perceptual filters in the RAS, automatically surfacing confirming evidence until the assumption appears to be objective fact."},{q:"What is the master diagnostic question used in the Professional Assumption Audit to uncover hidden beliefs?",options:["'Why are my colleagues holding me back?'","'What must I privately assume to be true about myself or this context for my recurring behavior to make logical sense?'","'What job title do I want in ten years?'","'How many hours did I work last week?'"],correct:1,explanation:"Reverse-engineering your actual recurring behaviors exposes the real operating assumption far more accurately than abstract self-reflection."},{q:"Why does 'Assumption Disruption' rely on logging concrete daily counter-evidence rather than repeating hollow affirmations?",options:["Because affirmations are illegal in corporate environments","Because the analytical Beta mind rejects hollow claims, whereas real counter-evidence retrains the RAS to scan for and validate the new Sovereign assumption","Because writing takes more time than speaking","Because counter-evidence is only shared with HR"],correct:1,explanation:"Feeding the RAS verifiable counter-evidence bypasses cynical resistance and builds a myelinated neural case for the new identity."}]},{id:"b03-l04",num:"04",title:"The 90-Day Shift Plan",duration:"25 min",type:"workshop",description:"A structured three-phase neuroplasticity protocol (Foundation, Application, Integration) for installing a new professional identity.",objectives:["Structure a 90-day identity shift across three 30-day neuroplastic phases: Foundation, Application, and Integration","Design daily micro-experiments that bridge the gap between your old reflex and your Sovereign identity","Install structural accountability rhythms that prevent allostatic drift back to the old comfort zone"],content:["Identity does not rewire during a single moment of seminar inspiration—it rewires through consistent, emotionally anchored repetition over 66 to 90 days until the new neural pathway achieves automaticity.","The Vibe Hyr 90-Day Shift Plan divides this neuroplastic journey into three 30-day phases: Phase 1: Foundation (Days 1–30), Phase 2: Application (Days 31–60), and Phase 3: Integration (Days 61–90).","During Foundation (Days 1–30), you run daily RAS counter-evidence logging, nightly Evening Revision, and low-stakes behavioral micro-experiments that prove the new Sovereign assumption is safe. During Application (Days 31–60), you take progressive exposure into high-stakes arenas—executive presentations, pricing/scope negotiations, direct boundary conversations—where the old archetype used to take over.","During Integration (Days 61–90), the new assumption transitions from deliberate practice to Default Mode Network baseline. Colleagues have now adjusted to your upgraded broadcast, social feedback reinforces your Sovereign posture, and the new standard becomes self-sustaining."],tool:"90-Day Shift Planner",lab:{title:"90-Day Shift Architect",subtitle:"Foundation \xb7 Application \xb7 Integration",scenario:"You are designing the next 90 days of your professional trajectory around one core Sovereign identity assumption.",prompt:"Map your 3-phase blueprint: (1) State your present-tense 90-Day Sovereign Identity Statement ('I am...'), (2) Define one daily Foundation micro-action for Days 1–30, (3) Define one high-stakes Application arena for Days 31–60, and (4) Name the accountability structure that will keep you on track through Day 90.",accentColor:i.purple},quiz:[{q:"What are the three sequential 30-day phases of the Vibe Hyr 90-Day Shift Plan?",options:["Reading, Memorizing, and Testing","Foundation (Days 1–30), Application (Days 31–60), and Integration (Days 61–90)","Planning, Waiting, and Celebrating","Disruption, Conflict, and Resolution"],correct:1,explanation:"Foundation builds initial neural evidence in low-stakes settings, Application tests the identity in high-stakes arenas, and Integration consolidates it as the default baseline."},{q:"Why is the shift protocol calibrated to 90 days rather than 3 or 7 days?",options:["Because corporate quarters are 90 days long","Because habit and identity neuroplasticity research (Lally et al.) shows automaticity and myelination require 66 to 90 days of consistent repetition","Because it takes 90 days to read the workbook","Because you only practice once a month"],correct:1,explanation:"Deep myelination and Default Mode Network identity consolidation require roughly 66–90 days of sustained practice to outcompete decades-old habits."},{q:"What is the primary focus during Phase 2: Application (Days 31–60)?",options:["Keeping the new identity completely secret and avoiding all meetings","Progressive exposure—deliberately bringing the new Sovereign assumption into higher-stakes professional contexts where the old archetype used to dominate","Retiring from your job","Starting a brand new 90-day plan from scratch"],correct:1,explanation:"Once Phase 1 establishes initial safety and counter-evidence, Phase 2 applies the Sovereign identity in high-visibility, high-stakes arenas."}]},{id:"b03-l05",num:"05",title:"Revision and the Law of Assumption",duration:"22 min",type:"video",description:"How to use nightly memory reconsolidation to rewrite limiting workplace interactions and install Sovereign authority.",objectives:["Explain the neuroscience of memory reconsolidation and why recalled memories become malleable before re-storage","Execute the 10-minute Evening Revision Protocol during the pre-sleep Alpha/Theta window","Pair backward-looking Revision with a forward-looking Identity Evidence Log for rapid self-concept shifts"],content:["Revision is not denial or wishful thinking—it is applied memory reconsolidation. Neuroscience research demonstrates that human memories are not static video files; every time you recall an event, the neural trace becomes temporarily labile (malleable) before being reconsolidated into storage.","When an interaction at work goes poorly—you froze in a meeting, over-explained defensively, or let a boundary get crossed—most professionals replay the embarrassment on loop that evening. Neurologically, that rumination re-etches the limiting identity deeper into the subconscious.","Neville Goddard's Revision technique intercepts this reconsolidation window. In the final 10 minutes before sleep (as your brainwaves slow into Alpha and Theta), you bring the misaligned interaction to mind and replay it vividly from the first-person perspective as the Sovereign version of yourself—hearing your calm, authoritative voice, feeling your grounded posture, and seeing the room respond with respect.","Because the subconscious in Theta encodes vividly felt imaginal scenes with the same emotional salience as physical events, nightly Revision neutralizes the old threat charge and installs a Sovereign behavioral template for tomorrow's meetings."],tool:"Evening Revision Protocol",lab:{title:"The Evening Revision Studio",subtitle:"Memory Reconsolidation in Action",scenario:"Recall a recent workplace interaction from the past two weeks where you left feeling smaller, reactive, or misaligned with how you wanted to show up.",prompt:"Write the Revised Sovereign Script of that exact moment in first-person present tense: What does your posture feel like? What exact words do you say with calm authority? How does the other person respond when met with your regulated Sovereign state?",accentColor:i.purple},quiz:[{q:"What documented neurobiological mechanism explains why the Evening Revision technique updates professional self-concept?",options:["Total amnesia of the workday","Memory reconsolidation—recalled memories enter a temporarily plastic state where their emotional charge and identity meaning can be updated before re-storage","Increasing cortisol levels right before sleep","Logical suppression in the Beta brainwave state"],correct:1,explanation:"During memory reconsolidation in the pre-sleep Alpha/Theta state, re-experiencing an event from the Sovereign identity neutralizes old threat encoding and wires a new response template."},{q:"What happens neurologically when a professional spends their evening ruminating and replaying a frustrating workplace interaction as it originally happened?",options:["They solve the problem faster","They repeatedly fire and myelinate the exact neural pathway of the old reactive state, reinforcing the limiting assumption for tomorrow","They clear cortisol from their bloodstream","Nothing—evening thoughts have no effect on workplace performance"],correct:1,explanation:"Ruminating on a bad interaction with emotional charge acts as reverse-SATS, deepening the myelination of the very pattern you want to stop."},{q:"How do the Evening Revision Protocol and the daily Identity Evidence Log work together?",options:["They are identical exercises done at lunch","Revision works backward to neutralize and rewrite past limiting encodings, while the Evidence Log works forward to train the RAS to notice present-day Sovereign wins","You only use the Evidence Log if Revision fails","They are only used once a year during performance reviews"],correct:1,explanation:"Together they form a bidirectional identity engine: Revision clears historical drag while the Evidence Log builds forward RAS momentum."}]},{id:"b03-l06",num:"06",title:"Live Self-Mastery Session",duration:"60 min",type:"live",isLive:!0,description:"Live facilitated coaching session and capstone integration for Track 03: Know Yourself, Lead Yourself.",objectives:["Audit your complete three-layer Identity Architecture Map and Four Archetypes profile","Stress-test your new Sovereign Identity Assumption against real workplace resistance scenarios","Lock in your signed Personal 90-Day Shift Commitment and nightly Revision ritual"],content:["This capstone module integrates all five Track 03 pillars—Identity Architecture, The Four Archetypes, The Assumption Audit, The 90-Day Shift Plan, and Evening Revision—into a cohesive self-leadership operating system.","In the live coaching session, you will workshop your Assumption Audit and 90-Day Shift Plan with a Vibe Hyr facilitator, identifying any subtle Performer, Protector, or Prover language hiding inside your goals and upgrading it to clean Sovereign certainty.","Whether you are preparing for your live cohort session or completing your self-guided capstone today, finalize your Personal 90-Day Shift Commitment in the Lab below and schedule your daily 2-minute morning Sovereign induction and 5-minute Evening Revision.","Complete the Track 03 Capstone Check below to record your completion of Know Yourself, Lead Yourself."],tool:"Personal 90-Day Shift Commitment",lab:{title:"Personal 90-Day Shift Commitment",subtitle:"Track 03 Sovereign Contract",scenario:"You are formalizing the 90-day identity shift that will govern your decisions, boundaries, and leadership presence for the next quarter.",prompt:"Write your formal commitment: (1) The old assumption & archetype I am retiring today, (2) My present-tense Sovereign Identity Assumption, (3) My daily morning & evening rituals (Evidence Log + Revision), and (4) The boldest Phase 2 action I commit to taking in the next 30 days.",accentColor:i.purple},quiz:[{q:"If a professional changes their calendar habits (Surface Layer) without changing their core belief that they are 'unworthy of executive authority' (Architectural Layer), what predicts their long-term outcome?",options:["The calendar app will permanently fix their confidence","The Architectural assumption will instruct the RAS to generate self-sabotage or burnout until outer circumstances snap back to match the inner self-concept","Their colleagues will promote them immediately","Nothing—self-concept has no effect on execution"],correct:1,explanation:"The Architectural identity assumption is the thermostat of professional performance; lasting change requires aligning the root assumption with the desired behavior."},{q:"When you catch yourself slipping into the Prover or Protector archetype mid-week during your 90-Day Shift Plan, what is the Sovereign response?",options:["Abandon the 90-Day Plan because you failed","Observe the archetype without shame, deploy Assumption Disruption with 3 pieces of counter-evidence, and revise the moment in that night's Evening Revision","Blame your manager for triggering you","Work through the weekend without sleeping"],correct:1,explanation:"Self-mastery is rapid recognition and reconsolidation—catching the old program without judgment and using Evidence + Revision to strengthen the Sovereign pathway."},{q:"What is the primary role of your Reticular Activating System (RAS) once you consistently inhabit your Sovereign Identity Assumption?",options:["To make your work completely effortless with zero challenges","To automatically filter for and surface the opportunities, relationships, and behavioral choices that confirm and compound your Sovereign authority","To block all emails from clients","To put you to sleep during daytime meetings"],correct:1,explanation:"Once the Sovereign assumption is installed, the RAS acts as an automated radar surfacing confirming evidence, strategic solutions, and high-value connections."}]}]},{id:"the-high-frequency-team",num:"04",tag:"Team Excellence",title:"The High-Frequency Team",subtitle:"Co-Regulation \xb7 Collective Assumptions \xb7 Accountability",color:i.teal,totalTime:"~3 hrs",description:"Sustained high performance is a byproduct of collective frequency. This track builds the organizational architecture for team co-regulation, shared assumptions, intrinsic accountability, and zero-drift maintenance.",lessons:[{id:"b04-l01",num:"01",title:"Team Frequency Mapping",duration:"18 min",type:"video",description:"Every team has a collective autonomic state. Learn to diagnose whether your group is running in Ventral Vagal, Sympathetic, or Dorsal Vagal mode.",objectives:["Define Team Frequency as an emergent neurobiological field shaped by autonomic co-regulation","Diagnose the behavioral markers of Ventral Vagal (generative), Sympathetic (defensive/frantic), and Dorsal Vagal (disengaged) teams","Leverage the leader's nervous system hierarchy to shift collective team state before meetings begin"],content:["Team frequency is the collective physiological and emotional baseline of a group. Just as an individual operates in one of three Polyvagal states, a team develops a shared autonomic climate that governs its creativity, candor, and resilience.","A Ventral Vagal team operates in safety and social engagement: ideas are challenged without personal threat, mistakes are surfaced early, and energy is generative. A Sympathetic team operates in chronic mobilization: meetings feel defensive or performative, urgency replaces strategy, and turf wars flare under deadline stress. A Dorsal Vagal team operates in shutdown: cameras and voices go quiet, compliance replaces initiative, and people count the hours until Friday.","Because of authority-weighted attunement, team frequency is disproportionately set by the most influential nervous system in the room. A leader who walks into a sprint review tight, rushed, and reactive pushes the entire room toward sympathetic defense within 90 seconds—before a single slide is shown.","Team Frequency Mapping uses real-time behavioral indicators—spontaneous dialogue quality, response to ambiguity, and conflict recovery speed—to diagnose where your team is actually operating and intervene at the physiological source."],tool:"Team Frequency Map",lab:{title:"Team Autonomic Climate Audit",subtitle:"Ventral Vagal \xb7 Sympathetic \xb7 Dorsal Vagal",scenario:"Think of your team's last two weekly meetings or project reviews. Look past what was on the agenda to the physiological climate in the room (who spoke, vocal tone, posture, how bad news or disagreement was handled).",prompt:"Diagnose which Polyvagal state (Ventral Vagal, Sympathetic, or Dorsal Vagal) dominated those sessions, identify two specific behavioral markers that prove your diagnosis, and describe the exact entry-state shift you will bring to the first 120 seconds of your next team meeting.",accentColor:i.teal},quiz:[{q:"What does nervous system co-regulation mean in a team environment?",options:["Everyone on the team has the exact same personality type","Team members' autonomic nervous systems continuously read and synchronize with one another—and disproportionately calibrate to the leader's physiological broadcast","Individual self-regulation is no longer necessary once you join a team","Only HR is responsible for team morale"],correct:1,explanation:"Through mirror neurons and authority-weighted neuroception, a team's collective threat-or-safety baseline entrains to the dominant physiological broadcast in the room."},{q:"A team where meetings are quiet, cameras or eyes are averted, risk-taking has stopped, and members do the bare minimum to avoid attention is exhibiting which collective Polyvagal state?",options:["Ventral Vagal (Safe & Generative)","Sympathetic (Mobilized Fight-or-Flight)","Dorsal Vagal (Immobilized Shutdown / Disengagement)","Peak Flow State"],correct:2,explanation:"Passive withdrawal, flat affect, and risk avoidance are classic markers of collective Dorsal Vagal shutdown following prolonged overwhelm or low psychological safety."},{q:"Why are lagging performance metrics alone insufficient for monitoring a team's health?",options:["Because numbers are too hard to calculate","Because a team can temporarily hit targets through Sympathetic burnout right before turnover and collapse—whereas Team Frequency Mapping catches autonomic drift months earlier","Because clients don't care about results","Because high-frequency teams never track metrics"],correct:1,explanation:"Sympathetic overdrive can mask cultural decay in short-term metrics until burnout or attrition hits; frequency mapping reads the leading physiological indicators."}]},{id:"b04-l02",num:"02",title:"Collective Assumptions",duration:"22 min",type:"video",description:"Teams don't just share goals — they share a collective RAS filter that sets their invisible performance ceiling.",objectives:["Define a Collective Assumption and explain how socially reinforced narratives program a team's shared RAS","Surface unspoken team assumptions around capability, cross-functional trust, and organizational agency","Install an upgraded Collective Assumption using shared behavioral experiments rather than top-down slogans"],content:["A team is a shared nervous system running on collective assumptions. Beyond official KPIs and OKRs, every group holds an unwritten consensus about 'who we are, what is actually possible here, and how this organization treats us.'","Just as an individual assumption programs the personal RAS, a collective assumption programs the team's shared perceptual filter. A team holding the assumption 'Executive leadership never backs our bold ideas' will unconsciously water down proposals, misread neutral questions as rejection, and socially police any new hire who shows too much initiative.","Conversely, a team that assumes 'We are the group that solves the hardest problems in this company' filters obstacles as puzzles rather than insults, shares knowledge rapidly, and recovers from setbacks with minimal friction.","You cannot overwrite a limiting collective assumption with a motivational poster or a top-down speech—cynical team nervous systems reject slogans instantly. Collective assumptions are updated by naming the old assumption openly without blame, co-authoring a new operational assumption, and running a 14-day shared experiment that generates undeniable group proof."],tool:"Team Assumption Audit",lab:{title:"Collective Assumption Upgrade",subtitle:"Rewiring the Shared RAS",scenario:"Your team has developed a subtle, cynical consensus around a recurring frustration (e.g., 'Other departments never give us what we need on time' or 'We don't have the bandwidth to innovate'). Whenever the topic comes up, people exchange knowing sighs.",prompt:"Name the exact limiting Collective Assumption running in your group, write the upgraded Collective Assumption that replaces it, and design one concrete 14-day team experiment that will generate first-hand evidence for the new belief.",accentColor:i.teal},quiz:[{q:"What does a team's Collective Assumption most directly determine?",options:["The office floor plan","The team's collective performance ceiling—by instructing their shared RAS on what possibilities, solutions, and evidence to notice or dismiss","The software tools the company purchases","Individual tax brackets"],correct:1,explanation:"Collective assumptions act as a socially enforced perceptual filter that determines how much effort, creativity, and resilience a group deploys."},{q:"Why do top-down motivational speeches usually fail to change a team's limiting collective assumption?",options:["Because the speeches are too short","Because collective assumptions are socially reinforced by shared experience; only making the assumption discussable and generating new shared behavioral evidence updates the group's filter","Because employees prefer emails over speeches","Because collective assumptions can never be changed"],correct:1,explanation:"Without surfacing the unspoken narrative and creating new lived evidence together, top-down slogans trigger skepticism and reinforce the old assumption."},{q:"How can a leader quickly spot a limiting collective assumption in their team?",options:["By looking at what the team writes in marketing brochures","By listening to the automatic 'knowing jokes,' shared sighs, and default explanations the team uses whenever a setback or ambitious target appears","By checking who arrives earliest in the morning","By counting the number of slides in a presentation"],correct:1,explanation:"Unspoken collective assumptions reveal themselves in the reflexive stories, cynical humor, and default attributions the group shares under pressure."}]},{id:"b04-l03",num:"03",title:"The Accountability Architecture",duration:"25 min",type:"workshop",description:"Move from imposed surveillance to intrinsic, identity-based accountability anchored in Self-Determination Theory.",objectives:["Distinguish between Imposed Accountability (surveillance/compliance) and Intrinsic Accountability (identity/ownership)","Design team structures that satisfy Autonomy, Competence, and Relatedness (Self-Determination Theory)","Facilitate a co-authored Team Accountability Agreement including a blameless Recovery Protocol for missed commitments"],content:["Imposed accountability requires a constant monitor; the moment the manager looks away, output drops to the minimum required for compliance. Intrinsic accountability is built into the team's collective identity: 'We do what we say because that is who we are to each other.'","Deci and Ryan's Self-Determination Theory proves that sustained intrinsic ownership requires three neurobiological nutriments: Autonomy (having a voice in how the work is structured), Competence (clear standards and visible mastery), and Relatedness (knowing my teammates genuinely have my back).","Surveillance-based management destroys all three—it crushes Autonomy through micromanagement, erodes Competence through punitive fault-finding, and replaces Relatedness with self-protection.","The Vibe Hyr Accountability Architecture replaces surveillance with a co-authored Team Accountability Agreement containing three pillars: (1) Explicit Shared Standards owned by peers, (2) Predictable Check-In Rhythms designed to unblock rather than interrogate, and (3) A Clean Recovery Protocol—when a commitment is at risk, team members surface it early, renegotiate transparently, and extract the systemic lesson without shame."],tool:"Team Accountability Agreement Template",lab:{title:"Intrinsic Accountability Architect",subtitle:"From Surveillance to Ownership",scenario:"A key deliverable on your team slipped last week. Nobody flagged the delay until the deadline had already passed, and when you asked about it, team members immediately pointed to external blockers and went defensive.",prompt:"Diagnose what part of the Accountability Architecture is missing (Autonomy, Competence, Relatedness, or a safe early-flagging Recovery Protocol), and write the exact 3-step Accountability Agreement you will co-create with the team so early risk-surfacing becomes a point of pride rather than fear.",accentColor:i.teal},quiz:[{q:"How does Intrinsic Accountability differ fundamentally from Imposed Accountability?",options:["Intrinsic accountability uses harsher financial penalties","Intrinsic accountability is embedded in the team's shared identity and peer agreements rather than relying on external managerial surveillance","Intrinsic accountability means deadlines no longer matter","Imposed accountability produces higher creativity and trust"],correct:1,explanation:"Imposed accountability breeds compliance and hiding; intrinsic accountability anchors ownership in who the team assumes itself to be."},{q:"According to Self-Determination Theory (Deci & Ryan), which three psychological needs must an accountability system support to generate intrinsic motivation?",options:["Fear, Competition, and Surveillance","Autonomy, Competence, and Relatedness","Speed, Volume, and Hierarchy","Bonuses, Titles, and Corner Offices"],correct:1,explanation:"When Autonomy, Competence, and Relatedness are supported by the team's architecture, intrinsic ownership and high performance flourish naturally."},{q:"Why must a high-frequency Team Accountability Agreement include an explicit 'Recovery Protocol' for when a commitment is at risk or missed?",options:["So the team knows who to fire first","Because when people know there is a safe, structured way to flag risk early and recover without shame, they stop hiding problems until it's too late","To lower performance standards so nobody ever feels challenged","To generate paperwork for legal compliance"],correct:1,explanation:"Most catastrophic project failures happen because fear of blame caused people to hide early warning signs. A clean Recovery Protocol rewards early transparency."}]},{id:"b04-l04",num:"04",title:"Communication at High Frequency",duration:"22 min",type:"workshop",description:"Install the 5-step Team Clarity Protocol to achieve a low friction-to-resolution ratio even under extreme deadline pressure.",objectives:["Measure your team's Friction-to-Resolution Ratio and understand why speed of repair matters more than conflict avoidance","Install the 5-step Team Clarity Protocol: Intention-Setting, Observation-First, Impact Transparency, Request Specificity, and Confirmation Loops","Build a high-safety developmental feedback culture where candor is experienced as investment rather than attack"],content:["High-frequency teams do not have zero conflict—artificial harmony is actually a symptom of Dorsal Vagal disengagement or Protector fear. What distinguishes an elite team is a low Friction-to-Resolution Ratio: friction is surfaced early, processed cleanly, and resolved into clarity within hours rather than festering for months.","Google's Project Aristotle and Dr. Amy Edmondson's research confirm that Psychological Safety—the shared belief that the team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking—is the #1 predictor of team excellence. Psychological safety is not 'being nice' or lowering standards; it is removing the social threat from candor so standards can be held higher.","To operationalize high-frequency candor, teams adopt the 5-step Clarity Protocol: (1) Intention-Setting ('My goal in raising this is to unblock Friday's launch, not to critique your effort'); (2) Observation-First framing; (3) Impact Transparency; (4) Request Specificity; and (5) Confirmation Loops ('To make sure we're locked in, here's what I have us agreeing to—does that match your read?').","When every team member shares this 5-step protocol, difficult feedback no longer triggers SCARF threat alarms—because the structure itself guarantees respect, clarity, and forward movement."],tool:"Team Communication Clarity Protocol",lab:{title:"The Friction-to-Resolution Accelerator",subtitle:"The 5-Step Team Clarity Protocol",scenario:"Two sub-teams on your project have been quietly frustrated with each other's handoff quality for three weeks, venting in side chats while staying polite and silent in the joint weekly sync.",prompt:"Write the exact 5-step Clarity Protocol script (1: Intention-Setting, 2: Verifiable Observation, 3: Shared Impact, 4: Specific Request, 5: Confirmation Loop) you will use to bring this friction onto the table and resolve it in under 10 minutes.",accentColor:i.teal},quiz:[{q:"What is the true hallmark of communication on a High-Frequency Team?",options:["They never disagree about anything","A low Friction-to-Resolution Ratio—they surface friction early and resolve it cleanly into clarity rather than letting it fester in side channels","Only the team leader is allowed to speak during meetings","All feedback is saved for annual written reviews"],correct:1,explanation:"Elite teams don't avoid friction; they use shared protocols and psychological safety to convert friction into alignment rapidly."},{q:"Why does Step 1 of the Team Clarity Protocol begin with explicit Intention-Setting ('My goal in sharing this is X, not Y')?",options:["To waste time before delivering bad news","Because pre-framing positive, collaborative intent disarms the listener's amygdala threat scan before critical content is delivered","Because corporate HR policies require a disclaimer","To avoid making a specific request"],correct:1,explanation:"Under stress, brains default to assuming critique is a Status or Relatedness attack. Explicit intention-setting signals safety so the prefrontal cortex stays open."},{q:"What is the purpose of Step 5: The Confirmation Loop at the end of an important team discussion?",options:["To repeat the entire 60-minute meeting word for word","To verify shared meaning by summarizing who owns what by when and checking that everyone's interpretation matches—eliminating phantom agreements","To test who was paying attention","To delay action until next week"],correct:1,explanation:"Most execution breakdowns come from 'phantom agreements' where two people leave the same meeting with different assumptions about what was decided."}]},{id:"b04-l05",num:"05",title:"Sustaining Team Excellence",duration:"20 min",type:"video",description:"All high-performing systems experience entropy without maintenance. Build the Daily, Weekly, Monthly, and Quarterly renewal architecture.",objectives:["Spot the five early warning signals of Team Frequency Drift before performance metrics decline","Install a 4-timescale Frequency Maintenance Calendar (Daily State Check, Weekly Pulse, Monthly Calibration, Quarterly Renewal)","Protect collective momentum against organizational pressure, leadership transitions, and sprint fatigue"],content:["By the laws of organizational entropy, every high-performing team drifts toward lower frequency if left on autopilot. Deadlines accumulate, micro-tensions go unrevised, and communication gradually slides from genuine candor back into performative updates.","The five early signals of Team Frequency Drift appear weeks before KPIs drop: (1) Meetings become slightly more formal and guarded; (2) Pronoun shift from 'we' to 'I/they'; (3) Spontaneous peer-to-peer help declines; (4) Minor Stage 1 frictions take days instead of minutes to clear; and (5) Energy in the final hour of the workday feels depleted rather than accomplished.","Sustaining team excellence requires a proactive Maintenance Architecture across four timescales: Daily (a 5-minute standup pairing task focus with a 1-word nervous system state check), Weekly (a 20-minute Pulse Review celebrating one high-frequency win and clearing one Stage 1 friction), Monthly (a 60-minute Frequency & Assumption Audit), and Quarterly (a half-day Renewal & Accountability Agreement refresh).","When maintenance is baked into the calendar as structural infrastructure rather than treated as an emergency repair after a crisis, high frequency becomes the team's permanent baseline."],tool:"Team Frequency Maintenance Calendar",lab:{title:"Anti-Drift Maintenance Architect",subtitle:"Daily \xb7 Weekly \xb7 Monthly \xb7 Quarterly",scenario:"Your team just finished a major high-pressure launch. Everyone is relieved, but you notice early drift signals: people are quieter in standups, 'we' language is slipping into 'my tickets,' and minor handoff friction is being ignored.",prompt:"Identify the drift stage and design your immediate 3-part Maintenance Intervention: (1) The 5-minute Daily State & Acknowledgment check you will open tomorrow with, (2) The Weekly Pulse question you will ask this Friday, and (3) The Monthly Renewal ritual you are putting on the calendar.",accentColor:i.teal},quiz:[{q:"Which of the following is an early leading indicator of Team Frequency Drift—appearing weeks before performance metrics visibly drop?",options:["A sudden shift in team language from 'we' to 'I/they,' increased meeting formality, and slower resolution of minor Stage 1 friction","The team completing projects ahead of schedule","Team members proactively offering help across roles","High psychological safety in retrospectives"],correct:0,explanation:"Pronoun shifts ('we' to 'I/they'), guarded formality, and lingering micro-friction are early neuro-cultural markers that collective ventral-vagal safety is eroding."},{q:"How is Team Frequency Drift most effectively prevented over the course of a year?",options:["Replacing half the team every six months","Waiting until a major crisis erupts and holding a mandatory weekend retreat","Installing proactive maintenance rhythms across four timescales (Daily, Weekly, Monthly, Quarterly) that catch and recalibrate drift at Stage 1","Sending daily automated reminders to work harder"],correct:2,explanation:"Just like physical hygiene, team frequency requires lightweight daily, weekly, monthly, and quarterly rhythms to prevent entropy from accumulating."},{q:"Why does the Daily 5-minute check-in include a brief nervous-system state share ('My bandwidth/state today is...') alongside task priorities?",options:["To turn every standup into a 2-hour therapy session","Because affect labeling and state transparency allow the team to co-regulate, adjust load intelligently, and avoid misinterpreting a tired teammate's brevity as hostility","To grade employees on their mood","To eliminate task tracking altogether"],correct:1,explanation:"Knowing a teammate's physiological bandwidth in 10 seconds prevents projection ('they're mad at me') and enables real-time co-regulation and support."}]},{id:"b04-l06",num:"06",title:"Live Team Excellence Workshop",duration:"60 min",type:"live",isLive:!0,description:"Live facilitated group workshop and series capstone for Track 04: The High-Frequency Team.",objectives:["Complete a live Team Frequency Map and Collective Assumption Upgrade with your cohort or team","Co-author and ratify your Team Accountability Agreement and 5-step Clarity Protocol","Install your 90-Day Team Frequency Maintenance Calendar to lock in series-wide organizational transformation"],content:["Welcome to the series capstone of the Vibe Hyr Business Training curriculum. Across four tracks and 24 modules, you have built the complete architecture of conscious workplace performance: Track 01 (Foundation & Vibe Hygiene), Track 02 (De-Escalation & Response Protocols), Track 03 (Sovereign Self-Mastery), and Track 04 (The High-Frequency Team).","In this live facilitated workshop, you bring all five Track 04 systems together into a deployable Team Frequency Action Plan—mapping your group's autonomic baseline, upgrading your core collective assumption, signing your Intrinsic Accountability Agreement, and locking in your 5-step Clarity Protocol.","Complete the Track 04 Capstone Lab and Final Assessment below to generate your Team Frequency Action Plan and earn your Track 04 & Full Series completion credentials."],tool:"Team Frequency Action Plan",lab:{title:"Team Frequency Action Plan",subtitle:"Track 04 & Series Capstone Blueprint",scenario:"You are synthesizing the entire Vibe Hyr Business curriculum into a 90-day operating blueprint for your team or organization.",prompt:"Draft your 4-pillar Team Frequency Action Plan: (1) Our upgraded Collective Identity Assumption, (2) Our #1 Intrinsic Accountability & Early-Recovery Agreement, (3) Our commitment to the 5-step Clarity Protocol & Stage 1 intervention, and (4) Our non-negotiable Weekly & Monthly Maintenance Rhythms.",accentColor:i.teal},quiz:[{q:"How do the four tracks of the Vibe Hyr Business curriculum (Foundation, De-Escalation, Self-Mastery, Team Excellence) function together as one system?",options:["They are four unrelated topics that can only be used one at a time","Individual awareness and Vibe Hygiene (Track 1) enable regulated De-Escalation (Track 2), which is anchored by a Sovereign Self-Concept (Track 3) and scaled into a self-sustaining High-Frequency Team culture (Track 4)","Only Track 4 matters for managers and only Track 1 matters for individual contributors","They are theoretical lectures with no daily workplace application"],correct:1,explanation:"Each track builds the neurobiological and identity foundation for the next—moving from individual regulation to dyadic de-escalation, internal authority, and collective team architecture."},{q:"When a High-Frequency Team encounters an unexpected market setback or missed milestone, what is their default sequence of response?",options:["Panic, assign blame to protect status, and hide the data from leadership","Regulate physiologically (PAUSE), invoke the blameless Recovery Protocol to surface facts early, re-anchor in their Collective Assumption, and execute a Clarity Frame adjustment","Cancel all team meetings for a month","Wait for an external consultant to tell them what to do"],correct:1,explanation:"High-frequency teams combine physiological regulation, psychological safety, intrinsic accountability recovery, and outcome-focused RAS filtering."},{q:"What is the ultimate indicator that the Vibe Hyr Business architecture has been fully integrated into a team's culture?",options:["Everyone memorizes neuroscience definitions for a test","High regulation, Stage 1 candor, Sovereign ownership, and mutual co-regulation operate as the team's automatic daily identity—even when pressure is high and the leader is not in the room","The team never has to communicate anymore","Meetings are replaced entirely by automated spreadsheets"],correct:1,explanation:"True cultural integration means the team's shared Default Mode Network and structural rhythms sustain high-frequency excellence autonomously."}]}]}]}};